We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t})={2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{({2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+{2}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435118 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435124 1069 "q-139054" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435124 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435124 1072 #uuid "1a5498dd-6e1c-5317-8d48-505ce94b33dc" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435124 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435119 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435125 1069 "q-139066" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435125 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,y=3t+2,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435125 1072 #uuid "310c6308-d59f-5c23-bf3f-94f16264b626" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435125 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3t+2)\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3t+2)\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{13}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435122 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435126 1069 "q-139055" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435126 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=2{t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435126 1072 #uuid "969f9c5c-90f0-5451-a64e-6e16b1103b2e" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435126 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+1)\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(6{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+1)\\sqrt{1+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435117 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435127 1069 "q-352092" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435127 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{t}{4},\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435127 1072 #uuid "b3bc80a6-ca15-5095-bc92-205f586735f6" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435127 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{e}^{t}]={e}^{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{t}{4}]={e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{[{e}^{t}]}^{2}+{[{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{4}]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{16}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{16}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recognizing that the term under the square root is a perfect square, we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}({e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}({e}^{3t}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}({e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u={e}^{t}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$  We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}({e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({u}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{u}{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{2}{4})-(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{28}{12}+\\frac{3}{12}] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{31}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{31\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435121 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435128 1069 "q-138972" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435128 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435128 1072 #uuid "d124471f-78aa-50f9-bfd3-0eadf2ea5bb7" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true] [17592186435128 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}={sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\cdot |2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{0}-2(1-{u}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{u}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\pi \\cdot {[u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =16\\pi (1-\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =16\\pi (\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534754 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0c3ac195-5a28-5c11-99ed-a06a6384cb26" 17592186435091 "answer-1889d211-580a-525d-8863-d0bc666144a5" 17592186435092 "answer-1c721d8c-9e46-58a3-95bb-3174862c4a01" 17592186435093 "answer-206381c1-a55a-5bbf-8129-7ade3cea9753" 17592186435094 "answer-217edb44-0fc7-5d6d-964e-6c05dca7cb50" 17592186435095 "answer-25838e3e-57a1-5fd9-8feb-f77678c5ccc6" 17592186435096 "answer-29c95824-df17-58a2-8aac-76df26add78b" 17592186435097 "answer-333420d8-3841-5986-a880-12239e3b0c86" 17592186435098 "answer-4b3a2be8-e4d5-5828-9be5-65cc4722dd19" 17592186435099 "answer-53eb5f8d-2c90-56b2-af3c-af00df5e8365" 17592186435100 "answer-579fa76c-1f64-56b5-b97c-4581e9fe53fb" 17592186435101 "answer-5f781be2-8bd2-520a-9a09-5f6c042226c2" 17592186435102 "answer-717f0fc3-0cb4-5e82-a464-045bfd255128" 17592186435103 "answer-867968e5-1470-5d6b-bbb2-c22bb39822ef" 17592186435104 "answer-8f4dc2f2-4b7b-5c53-9837-433249177feb" 17592186435105 "answer-95766dce-20ec-56a7-bf74-a65bab0ccb22" 17592186435106 "answer-97765c6f-bd17-5d3b-a5c6-230f3b86329e" 17592186435107 "answer-aa6427b7-b0e6-5e33-9aca-612cfab836af" 17592186435108 "answer-c1dbc8d7-24ce-50f6-8164-8b4e61b2fccd" 17592186435109 "answer-c63274df-6f1f-56f7-bcf0-d35e3a9b440e" 17592186435110 "answer-d33dff8a-de5a-5820-bbc2-521bfdade558" 17592186435111 "answer-d855760e-bbf6-5e09-b377-fb92b66bdfbb" 17592186435112 "answer-dcc1610f-6e04-5f46-8bfb-9596dcf2e267" 17592186435113 "answer-df954cef-dd13-5a8f-ac48-dd0715a02bf0" 17592186435114 "answer-e4e77fce-c9b3-50a6-aca4-e69c1a78f228" 17592186435115 "answer-fb1ce31b-4cfb-5eab-8d4f-6c08ab46d8c0" 17592186435116 "answer-field-019c2510-13c6-5cb5-adc7-f6823183b11a" 17592186435117 "answer-field-61ae82f2-6c7c-57cb-9b66-5fd5901e4df5" 17592186435118 "answer-field-9ebb6a09-3962-51d8-a359-86c686b5cd13" 17592186435119 "answer-field-a5dd24f4-4688-5465-a47d-3059c114e3fd" 175921864